course: biology
lesson: 125
title: Timon and Pumbaa: real meerkats and warthogs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Timon the meerkat and Pumbaa the warthog live alone together, slurp bugs and sing «Hakuna Matata», which really is Swahili for «no worries».
explain: A real meerkat would never live alone: meerkats live in mobs of up to about 50, sleep piled together in burrows, and take turns standing tall on their back legs as lookouts.
explain: A real warthog is a wild pig that mostly grazes short grass on its knees and digs up roots, eating insects only now and then, and it runs with its thin tail straight up like a flag.
explain: Timon and Pumbaa's friendship is not so far from the truth: on the savanna, banded mongooses, close cousins of meerkats, pick ticks off warthogs, and both animals gain.
explain: But the film mixes up Africa: «Hakuna Matata» is East African Swahili, while real meerkats live in the dry lands of southern Africa.
fact: Meerkats stand up tall on their back legs as lookouts for hawks and jackals, using the tail as a prop; the guard gives a quiet «all is well» call and a sharp alarm when danger appears.
fact: Meerkats live in groups called mobs or gangs of up to about 50, and a meerkat alone could not keep watch and feed at the same time.
fact: Meerkats are a kind of mongoose, not a kind of rat or squirrel.
fact: Meerkats eat beetles, grubs, spiders, lizards and scorpions; they bite off or avoid the stinger and can survive a little venom, and adults teach pups with dead scorpions first, then live ones whose sting has been removed.
fact: The dark fur round a meerkat's eyes cuts down glare from the bright desert sun, and meerkats can close their ears when they dig.
fact: Meerkats live in big burrows with many tunnels and sleep piled together, because deserts get cold at night.
fact: Warthogs are wild pigs that mostly eat grass, roots and bulbs, kneeling on tough pads on their wrists to graze very short grass.
fact: A running warthog holds its thin tail straight up, which may help its young follow it through tall grass, and it can run at about 50 km per hour.
fact: Warthogs often use old aardvark burrows and back in tail-first, so their tusks face the opening.
fact: Banded mongooses pick ticks off warthogs lying down; a friendship where both sides gain is called mutualism.
fact: A warthog has two pairs of tusks: big curved upper ones and short, razor-sharp lower ones that rub against the upper ones and stay sharp.
fact: A warthog's «warts» are thick bumpy pads that protect its face in fights.
fact: «Hakuna matata» means «no worries» in Swahili, «Simba» means lion and «Rafiki» means friend, but real meerkats live in southern Africa, not East Africa.
exercise: Why does a meerkat stand up tall on its back legs? | To act as a lookout for hawks and jackals | To reach the sweet fruit hanging in the low trees | To dance for the group | To dry its belly in the wind
exercise: What does a meerkat use as a prop when it stands up tall? | Its tail | Its big ears | A stick it finds | Another meerkat's head
exercise: How do real meerkats live? | In groups called mobs of up to about 50 | Alone, like Timon, each in its own little burrow | In pairs with a warthog | In huge herds of thousands
exercise: What kind of animal is a meerkat? | A kind of mongoose | A kind of desert rat | A kind of ground squirrel | A kind of small monkey
exercise: How does a meerkat eat a scorpion safely? | It bites off or avoids the stinger | It waits for the scorpion to fall asleep | It swallows it whole and quickly | It only eats the scorpion's shell
exercise: How do adult meerkats teach pups to eat scorpions? | They give them dead ones, then live ones with the sting removed | They leave the pups to learn alone | They show them pictures of scorpions drawn in the sand | They feed them only grass and seeds until they are fully grown
exercise: What do the dark patches round a meerkat's eyes do? | Cut down glare from the bright desert sun | Make it look big and scary to hawks flying overhead | Help it see colours better | Hide its eyes from scorpions
exercise: Why do meerkats sleep piled together in their burrow? | Deserts get cold at night and huddling saves heat | They have only one bed | They are scared of the dark and want to feel each other close | It is the only way to fit in
exercise: What do real warthogs mostly eat? | Grass, roots and bulbs | Long lines of bugs | Meat from lions' kills | Only fruit from trees
exercise: Why does a warthog kneel on its wrists? | To graze very short grass | To pray to the setting sun | To rest its sore, tired legs | To hide from passing lions
exercise: Why does a running warthog hold its tail straight up? | It may help its young follow it through tall grass | To steer like the rudder of a boat when it turns quickly | To scare off birds | To keep its tail out of the mud
exercise: Why does a warthog back into its hole tail-first? | So its tusks face the opening to fight off a predator | Because it cannot turn round in the dark narrow hole | To keep its tail warm | To sweep the hole clean
exercise: Why do mongooses climb over a warthog lying down? | To pick ticks off it, so both gain | To steal the grass it has just dug up | To sleep on its warm back | To fight it for the burrow
exercise: How many pairs of tusks does a warthog have? | Two pairs | Only one pair | Three pairs | None, they are horns
exercise: What does «Hakuna matata» mean in Swahili? | No worries | Long live the king | Let's go and eat | Good morning
exercise: Where do real meerkats live? | The dry lands of southern Africa | The deserts of Australia, far from Africa | The cold forests of northern Europe | The jungles of India
test: «meerkat on its own in my garden lol, like Timon» What is right? | Wild meerkats almost never live alone; they live in mobs | True, meerkats prefer to live alone, just like Timon in the film | True, they live with warthogs | True, they live in gardens
test: «3 warthogs, how many tusks altogether» Each has two pairs. How many? | 12 | 6 | 3 | 8
test: «5 meerkats on lookout standing up, how many back legs on the ground» How many? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: «Pumbaa eats bugs so warthogs eat bugs, duh» What is right? | Real warthogs mostly graze grass and dig roots; bugs only now and then | Right, they eat only bugs and grubs, just like Pumbaa in the film | Right, they slurp ants all day | Wrong, warthogs eat only meat
test: «warthogs have warts, gross, can u catch them» What is right? | They are not warts but bumpy pads protecting the face in fights | Yes, you catch warts from touching them, just like from toads | Yes, they are a skin disease | They are spots of dirt
test: What would happen if a meerkat mob had no lookout while feeding? | Hawks could catch them before they noticed | They would find more food safely | Nothing, meerkats are too fast to be hunted by anything | They would all fall asleep
test: «warthog's fat and slow, I can outrun it easy» What is right? | Warthogs can run at about 50 km per hour, faster than you | Right, they waddle slowly like ducks, so anyone can outrun them | Right, they cannot run at all | Right, they only crawl
test: «Timon is like a squirrel innit» What is right? | Meerkats are a kind of mongoose | Yes, a little desert squirrel that digs holes | Yes, a kind of rat | Yes, a small monkey
test: A cartoon shows meerkats in an East African rainforest singing in Swahili. What is real? | Swahili is spoken in East Africa, but meerkats live in southern Africa | Meerkats live in every rainforest in Africa and sing in the trees | Meerkats speak Swahili | Meerkats live only in Asia
test: A warthog meets a leopard in its burrow entrance. Why is it safer facing out? | It can charge out tusks first rather than be grabbed from behind | Leopards are scared of warthog tails and run off when they see one | It can hide its face in the soil | Facing out lets it sleep better
test: «meerkat just ate a scorpion and didn't die, how» What is right? | It bites off or avoids the stinger and can survive some venom | Scorpions are not real animals | Meerkats have metal teeth | The scorpion was made of plastic, as the real ones would kill it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the social life of the meerkat. | It lives in groups designated mobs, which may number up to approximately fifty individuals | It lives in solitude, in the manner of the character depicted in the film | It lives exclusively in association with a single warthog, with which it shares a burrow for life | It lives in herds numbering many thousands
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the warthog? | It consists principally of grasses, roots and bulbs, insects being taken only occasionally | It consists principally of insects, which are ingested in very great number each day | It consists principally of carrion, which is obtained from lions after they have eaten their fill | It consists exclusively of the fruits of trees
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the warthog enters its burrow in reverse. | Its tusks are thereby directed towards the entrance, whence a predator may approach | The burrow is of insufficient width to permit turning | Its tail is thereby protected from the cold of the night and from biting insects | The burrow is thereby cleansed of debris
exam: Ascertain the term which designates an association between two species from which both derive benefit, as between the banded mongoose and the warthog. | Mutualism | Predation | Hibernation | Migration
